      "id": "2121119443698979136",
      "text": "RT @TheCaptainEli: SpaceX is building a supercomputer near Brownsville, Texas.\n\nThe first job on site was cleaning up sixty years of other…",
      "created_at": "Fri Nov 13 05:54:18 +0000 2026",
      "lang": "en",
      "media": [],
      "retweet": {
        "id": "2121118479537394105",
        "text": "SpaceX is building a supercomputer near Brownsville, Texas.\n\nThe first job on site was cleaning up sixty years of other people's trash.\n\nScrap metal, rusting tanks, old appliances and thousands of illegal tires, hauled out of the mud.\n\nThink about that for a second.\n\nThis site will train frontier AI for medical research, emergency response, advanced manufacturing and national security.\n\nIt will make its own electricity, so the neighbors' power bills stay their own.\n\nSpaceX is also putting $220 million into Brownsville's water and wastewater system, adding 20 million gallons of capacity every day for the city.\n\nMore than 3,000 construction jobs already, with more than 500 permanent ones to come.\n\nElon Musk summed it up in three words: \"SpaceX community benefits.\"\n\nA company spends $15 billion on Starship and another $24 billion on a supercomputer in one corner of Texas when it plans to stay for decades.\n\nElon Musk builds the place up first, because he believes the future will be built right there.",
        "created_at": "Fri Nov 13 05:50:28 +0000 2026",
        "lang": "en",
        "media": [],
        "retweet": null,
        "quoted": {
          "id": "2120911969087607288",
          "text": "Development of the Starbase supercompute site is creating an opportunity to address decades of environmental neglect. \n\nPortions of the property were used for unauthorized dumping dating back to the 1960s. We're undertaking cleanup efforts to remove decades of accumulated debris, including scrap metal, discarded appliances, old tanks, fiberglass insulation, construction materials, and thousands of illegally dumped tires",
          "created_at": "Thu Nov 12 16:09:52 +0000 2026",
          "lang": "en",
