        "text": "In 2014, Elon Musk stood in front of an audience at MIT and told the whole world exactly where AI was going.\n\nEveryone missed it. Because he used one word, and that's the only thing the internet remembered: \"demon.\"\n\nThe headlines called him scared of killer robots. Tech blogs called it hyperbole. People moved on.\n\nI went back and watched that moment. It's barely 2 minutes long. You'll never hear the word \"AI\" the same way again.\n\nHere's what I took away.\n\n1. He called it before almost anyone.\n\nSomeone in the audience asks about AI. His face goes serious.\n\n\"If I were to guess like what our biggest existential threat is, it's probably that. So we need to be very careful with the artificial intelligence.\"\n\nThis was 2014. No ChatGPT. Most people had never heard the word \"chatbot.\"\n\n2. He asked for rules 12 years ago.\n\n\"I'm increasingly inclined to think that there should be some regulatory oversight, maybe at the national and international level, just to make sure that we don't do something very foolish.\"\n\nNational AND international. In 2014.\n\nGovernments are still arguing about it today.\n\n3. The demon line wasn't a joke.\n\n\"With artificial intelligence we are summoning the demon.\"\n\nAnd then the part almost nobody quotes:\n\n\"In all those stories where there's the guy with the pentagram and the holy water, it's like, yeah, he's sure he can control the demon. Doesn't work out.\"\n\nRead that again.\n\nHis warning wasn't about the demon. It was about the guy who thinks he can control it.\n\n4. Now here's what got me.\n\nIn 2014 he said: summoning the demon.\n\nIn 2018 he told Joe Rogan: \"Nobody listened.\"\n\nIn 2023 he signed a letter asking AI labs to pause.\n\nIn 2026 he told The Economist he can't see any way to stop it anymore. His new philosophy? \"Let's enjoy the ride.\" And: \"If you can't beat them, join them.\"\n\nToday he's building one of the most powerful AIs on Earth.\n\n12 years later, the man who warned us about the guy with the pentagram is holding one.\n\nMy takeaway:\n\nHe wasn't wrong in 2014. He just realized nobody was going to stop it. So he decided to build it himself.\n\nWhether that's genius or the exact ending of his own story, I honestly don't know.\n\nSo now I'm asking you:\n\nWas Musk right in 2014?\n\nOr did he become the guy in his own warning?\n\nTell me below. I genuinely want to know where people land on this.",
